#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046647} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519643} :activity/id #uuid "ce4873bc-0d3b-5f2c-a4a8-7ecb745b9327" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048943 :topic/id #uuid "cdbe026a-f2a4-5c0a-8b9f-a7d7b8060500" :topic/math-academy-id 625} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519643 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519589 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "31a4015c-7e0e-59a8-af52-660a728632ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519070} {:answer/id #uuid "3cc06de8-5221-52fa-920c-03c18a05eb1f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519088} {:answer/id #uuid "476e139f-e45f-5859-b5d8-144735e49388" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519105} {:answer/id #uuid "77c0f144-34b3-52df-ac81-d4928b60dca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519179} {:answer/id #uuid "89743f02-1d9f-5884-b7b0-ec422e07ca6d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519213}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519213} :answer-field/id #uuid "9725b865-33c5-5170-8ad4-3ac184e816c2" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519468}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc22d572-0b94-5462-9d85-2c1d61a16284" :question/math-academy-id "q-34999" :question/problem "![](images/39/39c2c86e1fca1dbcf80435237ba31fc4aed9ed8213578b4c5f3971408da59f2d.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=3$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. As $x$ approaches $0$ from the left and right we get\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{-}}f(x)=3,\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{0}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement II is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,{lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.$\n\n- Statement III is true.  We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\nHence, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=2.$ Meanwhile, \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{2}^{+}}f(x)=2\n$$\n\n \nHence ${lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x)=2.$ Therefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,0}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,2}f(x).$\n\nIn conclusion, only statement III is true."} :step/id #uuid "72d4130c-fc01-5da4-a860-034259704a88" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519666}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519651 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519516 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2c19089e-e70c-5d3e-89a1-8b1f43d5dfc9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519062} {:answer/id #uuid "5373c9c4-344c-5fdd-ab6a-8f94ae077c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519120} {:answer/id #uuid "78bc99e5-2323-56ec-9a3c-1a503b64e353" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519181} {:answer/id #uuid "8d69e6d5-8e64-51d5-9437-2837aa08c01e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=-\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519223} {:answer/id #uuid "c2b7b55b-e405-57e3-9cfb-bb0f23fba298" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519314}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519062} :answer-field/id #uuid "482e304e-d433-57bd-9c20-e198c527d454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186519436}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "069bdd21-6b0e-5746-9f9a-48c390ea491e" :question/math-academy-id "q-67539" :question/problem "![](images/1e/1e40e0ffa7880b3b3b63070f063de207dcfafbdbed5fdf02464e180feba715e2.png)\n\nThe function  $y=f(x)$ is defined on $x\\in (-\\frac{5}{2},3).$ Find ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x).$" :question/worked-solution "From the graph, we see that it is not possible to approach $x=3$ from the right. Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nWhen we approach $x=3$ from the left, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)=0\\,.\n$$\n\nSince ${lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}f(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x),$ we  conclude  that\n \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "96bbf527-f90f-58a7-9dde-fd51c996bbe9"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519666 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186078963 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d63a73ea-9815-5c7f-80c0-444c5b37376c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076557} {:answer/id #uuid "d7c90e00-5224-516b-81a4-8321e3f2f8f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076558} {:answer/id #uuid "20efce61-3023-5a01-b4d5-10268f69d6cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076559} {:answer/id #uuid "26be1b29-5328-5c08-aa6e-7aaaaf75c21d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076560} {:answer/id #uuid "4a335476-04ef-5f30-85b4-bca18e903569" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076561}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186076559} :answer-field/id #uuid "3fad43c5-0056-5e55-96e3-0ec6739ca78a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186076556}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "91103606-4eee-5423-99c0-f8331e850732" :question/math-academy-id "q-47596" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9e89a9b811bdc6b766b01eecf817df48968e3ea604b89bc55bc9660331194e0.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true concerning the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is shown above? \n\nI. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=4$\n\nII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}$\n\nIII. ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1$" :question/worked-solution "Let's analyze each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,-{1}^{+}}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,-1}f(x)=3.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Both limits ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)$ and ${lim}_{x\\to \\,{1}^{+}}f(x)$ do not exist because the function $f(x)$ is not given in the interval $(0,2].$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=\\text{DNE}\\,.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. We see from the graph that \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{-}}f(x)={lim}_{x\\to \\,{3}^{+}}f(x)=1\\,.\n$$\n\nTherefore, ${lim}_{x\\to \\,3}f(x)=1.$\n\nIn conclusion, only statements II and III are true."} :step/id #uuid "d31b4e41-aaa1-5ef0-a230-367e59083067" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186519651}}] :activity/title "Finding the Existence of a Limit Using One-Sided Limits" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186518988}]}
